WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.000
Okay, move out.

00:30.000 --> 00:48.680
All right, men, keep an eye on them now.

00:48.680 --> 00:51.680
We don't want them getting in our way.

00:51.680 --> 00:54.640
Hey, this is Bob Reddy with your National Guard Hope.

00:54.640 --> 00:59.240
And I want to tell you that the story of today's Army and Air National Guard is something all

00:59.240 --> 01:01.240
Americans can be proud of.

01:01.240 --> 01:06.240
Okay, men, they're getting too close.

01:06.240 --> 01:10.240
Let them have it.

01:10.240 --> 01:15.240
Look at them run.

01:15.240 --> 01:23.240
Get in our way, will they?

01:23.240 --> 01:30.240
That'll teach them.

01:30.240 --> 01:32.240
Is the coast clear?

01:32.240 --> 01:41.240
All right.

01:41.240 --> 01:42.240
Thank you, men.

01:42.240 --> 01:43.240
Hold it.

01:43.240 --> 01:44.240
Hold it, men.

01:44.240 --> 01:56.240
Hold it.

01:56.240 --> 01:57.240
They're not really running away, folks.

01:57.240 --> 02:00.240
They're just moving into position for some sharpshooting.

02:00.240 --> 02:02.240
At the rate they're moving, they'll be there in no time.

02:02.240 --> 02:08.240
These M-42s can move along at 40 miles per hour, and that's really hustling for babies this size.

02:08.240 --> 02:15.240
You know, the story of any military organization is found in its men, in its machines, and in its missions.

02:15.240 --> 02:20.240
These men are Virginia Army National Guardsmen, and that's a radio-controlled aerial target,

02:20.240 --> 02:22.240
better known to these guys as an RCAT.

02:22.240 --> 02:28.240
Their mission is to shoot it down.

02:28.240 --> 02:33.240
Once this little bird gets airborne, it's up to the RCAT controller to keep it flying on its prescribed course.

02:33.240 --> 02:37.240
You might say he's one pilot who flies with both feet on the ground.

02:37.240 --> 02:44.240
When these guys open up on that RCAT, they have the capability of pumping out 120 rounds per minute from each tube or barrel.

02:44.240 --> 02:48.240
Man, that's 240 rounds per minute.

02:48.240 --> 02:51.240
I'm glad they're on our side.

02:51.240 --> 02:55.240
Incidentally, the weapon I've been referring to is the M-42 Duster.

02:55.240 --> 03:01.240
Its heyday was a generation ago during World War II when it dusted enemy aircraft from the skies.

03:01.240 --> 03:07.240
After World War II, the Army gave the entire Duster system to the Army National Guard.

03:07.240 --> 03:11.240
The Guardsmen took the weapon, trained on it, and became professionally competent with it.

03:11.240 --> 03:29.240
Watch them go after this RCAT.

03:29.240 --> 03:30.240
See what I mean?

03:30.240 --> 03:37.240
That's what I call coming in for a soft landing.

03:37.240 --> 03:45.240
Makes things a little softer in our pocketbooks, too.

03:45.240 --> 03:51.240
If you've been thinking that Guardsmen face a paper enemy in the field on these training exercises, don't say it out loud.

03:51.240 --> 04:00.240
The Guard uses its own aggressors right from its own ranks to oppose the good guys on field exercises like this one at Camp Grayling, Michigan.

04:00.240 --> 04:03.240
Coordination is a key factor in any exercise.

04:03.240 --> 04:08.240
These Guardsmen are checking the lay of the terrain prior to the exercise.

04:08.240 --> 04:13.240
Battle planning strategy is just as important to the bad guys as it is to the heroes.

04:13.240 --> 04:22.240
These men are jumping off on an attack which will test the reaction and effectiveness of elements of the 107th Armored Cavalry Regiment of the Ohio Army National Guard.

04:22.240 --> 04:27.240
The guerrillas have funny going for them, as you can see.

04:27.240 --> 04:35.240
These scenes are typical of the realistic combat conditions Guardsmen undergo at training sites throughout the United States as well as overseas.

04:35.240 --> 04:58.240
Things could get a little hot around here.

04:58.240 --> 05:05.240
Gotta hand it to him, though. He really knows how to use that oversized cigarette lighter.

05:05.240 --> 05:12.240
I don't know about you, but as for me, I'm not too sure what I'd just as soon be on the aggressor's side in this battle.

05:12.240 --> 05:18.240
I'm just kidding. It's mighty comforting to know the bad guys are really good guys in this case.

05:18.240 --> 05:23.240
They're all part of the National Guard team. It's red, white, and blue as Old Glory herself.

05:23.240 --> 05:27.240
Now, if heavier reinforcements were needed, the Guard has them, too.

05:27.240 --> 05:31.240
This honest John Rocket can deliver a mighty punch if needed.

05:31.240 --> 05:34.240
Manning these powerful rockets takes a lot of training, believe me.

05:34.240 --> 05:37.240
But these Guardsmen are well-trained and well-prepared.

05:37.240 --> 05:42.240
The Army National Guard is composed of many different type units, including airborne and special forces.

05:42.240 --> 05:48.240
Organizations that demand the best in courage and stamina.

05:48.240 --> 05:54.240
These West Virginia Army Guardsmen are preparing to board a West Virginia Air Guard plane for a challenging night operation.

05:54.240 --> 06:03.240
Because of the nature of their military responsibilities, National Guardsmen don't accept the words professionalism and readiness as mere slogans.

06:03.240 --> 06:08.240
That guy reminds me of Crosby trying to get to the bank with all his loot.

06:08.240 --> 06:11.240
There's no room for the timid in an outfit like this, no sir.

06:11.240 --> 06:16.240
The important thing to remember is that Guardsmen have accepted their responsibilities readily.

06:16.240 --> 06:18.240
They're all volunteers.

06:18.240 --> 06:22.240
Men who have stood up, ready to be counted of their country need.

06:22.240 --> 06:29.240
By their oath, they're pledged irrevocably to safeguard the nation and its institutions against all enemies, foreign and domestic.

06:29.240 --> 06:34.240
Any Guardsman who is worthy of the name accepts this as a fact of service.

06:34.240 --> 06:38.240
Guardsmen are citizen soldiers and their purpose is the American purpose.

06:38.240 --> 06:48.240
For more than 300 years now, theirs has been an unbroken record of duty accomplished, of lives saved, of property secured, and of order in the face of potential chaos.

06:48.240 --> 06:54.240
Because of the close ties of the Army and the Air National Guard, they are working together continuously.

06:54.240 --> 07:02.240
These Rhode Island Army Guardsmen, for example, are practicing an infiltration operation with the help of their Rhode Island Air Guard buddies.

07:02.240 --> 07:10.240
The Air Guard has four of these Air Commando units, two of which are equipped with this HU-16 amphibian aircraft.

07:10.240 --> 07:20.240
In situations where Commando units require either a land or sea rendezvous or both, this sturdy flying boat can maneuver about and land almost anywhere with ease.

07:20.240 --> 07:25.240
Guardsmen are trained to operate under all climatic conditions over all types of terrain.

07:25.240 --> 07:32.240
Because the Guard is dispersed in communities throughout the United States, you'll find that many men are already skilled in many of their military assignments

07:32.240 --> 07:40.240
and they never raise their hand to take the oath as a member of the Guard, and that saves dollars for the taxpayers.

08:02.240 --> 08:16.240
Natural wild surroundings of the West Yellowstone area set the scene for these Montana Special Forces Guardsmen as they worked their way to the top of this barren outcropping.

08:16.240 --> 08:22.240
Ordinarily, this cliff would be considered a dangerous obstacle, but danger is just another word to these guys.

08:22.240 --> 08:33.240
They figured that the best way around is over the side. Some people call this a gung-ho gravitational go-mobile.

08:33.240 --> 08:42.240
Let's watch this fellow.

08:42.240 --> 08:53.240
Oh, now that wasn't bad. However, if you'd rather take the express route. Man, that's what I'd call moving.

08:53.240 --> 08:59.240
These guys are on the move too. Graceful movements, cat-like responses, superb balance and control.

08:59.240 --> 09:06.240
That's what it takes for a ballet like this. Yet those are the talents of these Montanans that contribute to their capabilities as Guardsmen.

09:06.240 --> 09:09.240
They're a proud bunch, and they've got a right to be.

09:09.240 --> 09:13.240
Now, it's pretty nice to get paid for this type of thing, but it's training with a purpose.

09:13.240 --> 09:18.240
They're actually on a mission to ambush a mythical enemy outpost.

09:18.240 --> 09:26.240
Once they reach the area of operations, a brief planning session is all that's needed to finalize the plan of attack.

09:26.240 --> 09:30.240
Strict silence becomes the order of the day as the Guardsmen close in on their objective.

09:30.240 --> 09:36.240
They know the outpost is somewhere close. Then, without warning, they spot the enemy just a few yards away.

09:36.240 --> 09:41.240
Because silence is imperative in a situation like this, the usual weapons are not employed.

09:41.240 --> 09:49.240
The quiet deadliness of a swift arrow does a much better job.

09:49.240 --> 09:58.240
Don't be alarmed. It was just a dummy.

09:58.240 --> 10:03.240
From the deep snows of Montana to the tropical beauty of Hawaii,

10:03.240 --> 10:07.240
that gives you some idea of the Guard's scope of geographical operations.

10:07.240 --> 10:10.240
They reach into Alaska and Puerto Rico, too.

10:10.240 --> 10:24.240
These Hawaii Guardsmen are on a routine patrol in an exercise that will eventually bring aerial support from mainland air guard units.

10:24.240 --> 10:28.240
A last-minute check of plans is made before the troops move out to the objective.

10:28.240 --> 10:33.240
You know, the Guard is composed of many veterans of World War II, of Korea, and even Vietnam.

10:33.240 --> 10:40.240
Their background and know-how provides a measurement of leadership that should serve as comfort to any parent whose son has joined the Guard.

10:40.240 --> 10:43.240
These veteran Guardsmen know the ropes. They've had battle experience.

10:43.240 --> 10:51.240
They've been leading men for years, and they know their business, including the techniques of camouflage.

10:51.240 --> 11:02.240
See anything? Well, watch.

11:02.240 --> 11:06.240
You hear a lot about mortar fire in Vietnam. Well, this is what a mortar looks like.

11:06.240 --> 11:12.240
Guardsmen are trained on them, as well as every other fire system with which there are.

11:12.240 --> 11:17.240
Here's a grenade launcher. It's an accurate little weapon. Just watch.

11:17.240 --> 11:27.240
Bullseye. And if you really want to clobber a target, grab one of these 106-millimeter recoilless rifles.

11:27.240 --> 11:37.240
These Guardsmen operate this weapon with perfection.

11:37.240 --> 11:40.240
Bullseye again. Really packs a wallop, doesn't it?

11:40.240 --> 11:44.240
If anyone thinks it's all sunshine and flowers in Hawaii, just look at this.

11:44.240 --> 11:48.240
This is the Air Guard's radar station atop Mount Koala.

11:48.240 --> 11:57.240
Nestled in virgin forests and frequently invaded by wild pigs, this outpost 4,000 feet high is shrouded in fog much of the time.

11:57.240 --> 11:59.240
That doesn't bother these Hawaiians, however.

11:59.240 --> 12:07.240
That radar sweeps through that fog and reaches miles in all directions of the Pacific in surveillance against a possible enemy attack.

12:07.240 --> 12:13.240
Nearby, other Guardsmen receive and interpret the information collected by the radar atop of the mountain.

12:13.240 --> 12:20.240
This Guardsman is a specialist, and each knows his responsibility like this fellow writing backwards on a transparent wall.

12:20.240 --> 12:23.240
If you want some fun, try that sometime.

12:23.240 --> 12:29.240
This man has located some Air Guard aerial tankers and jet fighters who are flying from the mainland.

12:29.240 --> 12:34.240
They're about 200 miles out, and the radar shows that they're about to rendezvous for the fighter refueling.

12:34.240 --> 12:41.240
Then they will continue to Hawaii, where they will take part in exercises with the active Army and with the Army Guard.

12:41.240 --> 12:46.240
The Air Guard has five air refueling units equipped with a KC-97 aircraft.

12:46.240 --> 12:51.240
With four reciprocating engines and two jet engines, these birds really get out of move.

12:51.240 --> 12:59.240
These tankers from the Illinois, Ohio, and Wisconsin Air Guard provide the fuel for the jet fighters making the deployment.

12:59.240 --> 13:03.240
They're F-84s from the Ohio Air National Guard.

13:03.240 --> 13:06.240
Imagine meeting a couple thousand miles over the ocean to gas up.

13:06.240 --> 13:17.240
It's a bit different from pulling into the corner service station.

13:17.240 --> 13:24.240
This refueling capability within the Guard makes it possible for the Guard's fighter units to deploy anywhere in the world in a matter of hours.

13:24.240 --> 13:27.240
These Air Guardsmen are operationally ready, too,

13:27.240 --> 13:37.240
and that means that they're ready for combat the minute they get where they're going.

13:57.240 --> 14:13.240
After landing at Hickam Air Force Base, the Guardsmen prepare to launch a mission to the island of Hawaii.

14:13.240 --> 14:17.240
They get a thorough briefing on their mission and pinpoint the target areas.

14:17.240 --> 14:20.240
These are your citizen airmen.

14:20.240 --> 14:24.240
These air crews meet the same standards as the active Air Force air crews do.

14:24.240 --> 14:30.240
They do not ask, nor do they receive, any credit for performing their roles as part-time airmen.

14:54.240 --> 15:01.240
Most of these men are successful businessmen in their home communities,

15:01.240 --> 15:12.240
and the National Guard believes that it is highly unlikely that a citizen soldier or citizen airman would demonstrate qualities as a soldier or airman that he does not possess as a citizen.

15:12.240 --> 15:15.240
This operation was known as Coptic Lightning.

15:15.240 --> 15:19.240
A good example of the support the Guard provides to active military service.

15:19.240 --> 15:24.240
These fighters were working with Army ground troops, and as part of their training for combat,

15:24.240 --> 15:28.240
they participate in operations in which close air support is demonstrated.

15:28.240 --> 15:53.240
Because the Air Guard is able to support these missions, the Air Force can turn its attention to higher priority missions.

15:53.240 --> 16:22.240
Trained for all combat tactics, these fighter pilots carry out airstrikes with speed and efficiency.

16:22.240 --> 16:37.240
Each sortie is planned and coordinated with precision.

16:37.240 --> 16:40.240
The Guard works closely with other services, too.

16:40.240 --> 16:48.240
These RF-84s of the Alabama Air Guard are taking off on a photo reconnaissance mission over Puerto Rico in Exercise Clove Hitch,

16:48.240 --> 16:58.240
while another plane has just returned with a load of film for immediate processing.

16:58.240 --> 17:03.240
Clove Hitch was a massive operation in the Caribbean involving all the services.

17:03.240 --> 17:10.240
Air Guard jet fighters, reconnaissance aircraft, and aerial tankers join these Navy men from the aircraft carrier Forestall,

17:10.240 --> 17:31.240
proving once again the teamwork among the services provides the background for effective defense whenever and wherever needed.

17:31.240 --> 17:38.240
Marines were participants, too. They came ashore by sea and air.

17:38.240 --> 17:54.240
Here they are engaging in an air assault after being spat from ships at sea to reinforce infantry elements ashore.

17:54.240 --> 17:58.240
All Guardsmen swear allegiance to the state and to the nation.

17:58.240 --> 18:06.240
For that reason, they train not only for their federal mission, but for their state mission as well.

18:06.240 --> 18:11.240
These Guardsmen are playing the part of rioters in this training session.

18:11.240 --> 18:17.240
In the event police forces are overwhelmed, the National Guard is the only force immediately available to a community

18:17.240 --> 18:26.240
for the distasteful assignment of quelling civil disorder.

18:26.240 --> 18:31.240
But the Guard has had to face the real thing on many occasions lately.

18:31.240 --> 18:39.240
In most cases, folks have recognized the unpleasantness of the duty, and they've accepted the Guard as a force for the preservation of law and order.

18:39.240 --> 18:46.240
Whether you're young or old, however, facing a shouting mob or a lurking sniper calls for the highest degree of discipline.

18:46.240 --> 18:55.240
This is what the Guardsmen faced in Cicero, Illinois, when demonstrators marched.

18:55.240 --> 19:00.240
These are scenes from the Watts riot where two divisions of Guardsmen were called to duty.

19:00.240 --> 19:08.240
The usual fire damage and destruction followed us in each riot, while patrolling Guardsmen surveyed troubled streets searching for snipers.

19:08.240 --> 19:14.240
Watch closely now. This is a spectacular shot of a fireman just as he's hit by sniper fire.

19:14.240 --> 19:23.240
It's a dangerous, frightening duty that Guardsmen face up to this responsibility as they face up to all others.

19:23.240 --> 19:29.240
Because of the increased intensity of violence in our cities, the Guard has accelerated its riot control training.

19:29.240 --> 19:35.240
This was Detroit, the highest level of damage and violence ever experienced in an American city.

19:35.240 --> 19:42.240
Fires and lots of ruined personal property, snipers, looting, and more destruction.

19:42.240 --> 19:51.240
The protection of personal property always has its problems, especially when the trouble spreads like quicksilver over an area plagued by hidden dangers.

19:51.240 --> 19:56.240
Two Guardsmen were assigned to every fire truck to protect the firemen from sniper fire.

19:56.240 --> 20:04.240
They were mighty busy on duty for long hours with little rest as they helped the weary firefighters battle more than 800 fires.

20:04.240 --> 20:12.240
Whenever a flood, tornado, or other disaster strikes a community, the Guard is usually there.

20:12.240 --> 20:23.240
This flood brought out the Guard in Kansas where the Guardsmen played an important role in rescuing the stranded and protecting property.

20:23.240 --> 20:28.240
The Guardsmen were on the scene immediately, aiding those who suffered in the tragedy in whatever way they could.

20:28.240 --> 20:32.240
Ever wonder who fills those sandbags that lie on the shores of an angry river?

20:32.240 --> 20:36.240
Ever wonder how those sandbags get on the riverbank?

20:36.240 --> 20:38.240
You're looking at the answer to both questions.

20:38.240 --> 20:42.240
Those bags weigh 40 to 50 pounds each.

20:42.240 --> 20:45.240
Every means is used to reach the stricken who need help.

20:45.240 --> 20:47.240
Trucks slowly search out victims.

20:47.240 --> 20:58.240
Guard helicopters do their share to assist in rescuing persons stranded in remote areas.

20:58.240 --> 21:01.240
Few tragedies strike with more fury than a howling tornado.

21:01.240 --> 21:05.240
This one leveled half of Oak Lawn, Illinois.

21:05.240 --> 21:08.240
Fifty lives were lost during this freaking incident.

21:08.240 --> 21:16.240
Guardsmen were on the job immediately, searching debris, helping out the wounded, assisting firemen fighting fires, and restoring power and communications.

21:16.240 --> 21:19.240
This school was all but demolished.

21:19.240 --> 21:26.240
Shattered ruins offer mute resistance to the presence of patrolling Guardsmen, alert to the possibility of looters.

21:26.240 --> 21:36.240
Because the Guard plays a vital role in providing medical assistance in these disasters, nurses are a necessary part of both the Army and Air National Guard.

21:36.240 --> 21:49.240
The Air Guard flies many aeromedical missions for the Air Force, and its aeromedical personnel have comforted many victims of the Vietnam War.

21:49.240 --> 21:53.240
Assisted by the medics, these nurses fly missions around the world.

21:53.240 --> 21:58.240
They work around the clock when necessary to comfort the sick, disabled, and wounded.

21:58.240 --> 22:03.240
This specialist helps the nurse check oxygen equipment aboard this C-97.

22:03.240 --> 22:10.240
If you really want to find out how terrific these people are, just ask some of the boys who've been wounded in Vietnam.

22:10.240 --> 22:14.240
This is Captain Betty Aiken, the Tennessee Air National Guard.

22:14.240 --> 22:17.240
She's made more than 23 trips to Vietnam.

22:17.240 --> 22:20.240
Here she administers aid to an air evacuated patient.

22:20.240 --> 22:25.240
Air Guard nurses receive specialized training so they can meet in-flight emergencies.

22:25.240 --> 22:30.240
I understand you have a pretty bad back injury. Have you ever flown before?

22:30.240 --> 22:35.240
Yeah, with good-looking nurses like that around, who wants to be healthy?

22:35.240 --> 22:40.240
What they need are more beautiful nurses like me.

22:40.240 --> 22:46.240
Yeah, we save your kind for us taxpayers.

22:46.240 --> 22:51.240
Much has been said and written concerning the tradition and heritage of the National Guard.

22:51.240 --> 22:55.240
Throughout its long history, the Guard has always attracted men of the highest caliber,

22:55.240 --> 23:00.240
many of whom have gone on to become great leaders and respected figures of prominence in the world.

23:00.240 --> 23:04.240
For example, George Washington, one of our early militiamen.

23:04.240 --> 23:07.240
Others make an impressive list. Here are just a few.

23:07.240 --> 23:12.240
Abraham Lincoln, yes sir, old honest age.

23:12.240 --> 23:20.240
Our 16th president. He started with the militia in 1832 and became a captain with the 4th Regiment of the Illinois Volunteers.

23:20.240 --> 23:26.240
Harry S. Truman, our 33rd president. Served as a World War I artillery officer,

23:26.240 --> 23:31.240
commanding Battery D of the 135th Field Artillery, Missouri National Guard.

23:31.240 --> 23:36.240
Charles A. Lindberg, hero of the aviation world,

23:36.240 --> 23:41.240
who won fame with the first solo flight over the Atlantic Ocean in 1927.

23:41.240 --> 23:46.240
Lindy's military career began in the 110th Observation Squadron, Missouri National Guard.

23:46.240 --> 23:50.240
He was granted leave to make his flight.

23:50.240 --> 23:56.240
General Earl G. Wheeler, the top military man in the United States as chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.

23:56.240 --> 24:01.240
Began his military service as a member of the District of Columbia Army National Guard.

24:01.240 --> 24:05.240
General Curtis LeMay, former Chief of Staff of the Air Force.

24:05.240 --> 24:11.240
He started his illustrious career in the Ohio Army National Guard as an artilleryman.

24:11.240 --> 24:18.240
Brigadier General Charles W. Sweeney, Deputy Chief of Staff for Air, Massachusetts Air National Guard.

24:18.240 --> 24:22.240
He piloted the B-29 that dropped the second atomic bomb on Japan.

24:22.240 --> 24:25.240
Its target, Nagasaki.

24:25.240 --> 24:30.240
Brigadier General Joseph J. Foss, South Dakota Air National Guard.

24:30.240 --> 24:35.240
Former governor of that state. A top ace who won the Medal of Honor in World War II.

24:35.240 --> 24:41.240
Captain Larry B. Mason, veteran of Vietnam who joined the Michigan Air National Guard.

24:41.240 --> 24:45.240
Awarded the Air Force Cross for extraordinary heroism in Vietnam.

24:45.240 --> 24:52.240
While attacking an enemy target, his B-57 tactical jet bomber was hit repeatedly.

24:52.240 --> 24:56.240
With a wounded navigator aboard, one engine shut down and the other one on fire.

24:56.240 --> 25:00.240
Approximately 30 square feet of surface area missing from the right wing.

25:00.240 --> 25:03.240
And most of the electrical system knocked out.

25:03.240 --> 25:08.240
This young pilot nursed his plane to a safe landing in friendly territory.

25:08.240 --> 25:13.240
The Guard also numbers prominent figures in the fields of entertainment and sports among those in its ranks.

25:13.240 --> 25:20.240
Here is Bobbie Rydell, popular singing and recording star, seen here performing at the Caribbe Hilton in San Juan, Puerto Rico.

25:20.240 --> 25:25.240
Bobbie is a specialist fourth class in the Pennsylvania Army National Guard.

25:25.240 --> 25:28.240
Bob Hays, teammate at Lillies with the Cowboys.

25:28.240 --> 25:30.240
All pro offensive flanker.

25:30.240 --> 25:35.240
Member of the Texas Army National Guard. He's known as the world's fastest swimmer.

25:35.240 --> 25:37.240
You can see why.

25:37.240 --> 25:45.240
♪♪♪

25:45.240 --> 25:49.240
Gary Anderson, Lieutenant in the California Army National Guard.

25:49.240 --> 25:51.240
His specialty, shooting.

25:51.240 --> 25:57.240
He's the guy who set three new records in winning the World's Rifle Championship in Cairo in 1962.

25:57.240 --> 26:01.240
Won two gold medals in the 1963 Pan Am Games.

26:01.240 --> 26:06.240
Set a new world record and picked up a gold medal in the Olympics at Japan in 1964.

26:06.240 --> 26:13.240
And won seven gold medals during 1966, firing in international shooting union sponsored events.

26:13.240 --> 26:16.240
Gary, by the way, is studying for the ministry.

26:16.240 --> 26:20.240
If you think the sky's the limit for ex-guardsmen, you're absolutely right.

26:20.240 --> 26:22.240
And here are some of the men who can prove it.

26:22.240 --> 26:25.240
Russell L. Schweickart, better known as Rusty.

26:25.240 --> 26:28.240
Former member of the Massachusetts Air National Guard.

26:28.240 --> 26:33.240
And the first guardsman to be selected for astronaut training directly from the Guard.

26:33.240 --> 26:36.240
You're watching him at work in a zero gravity situation.

26:36.240 --> 26:40.240
Hiring out bugs so future space flights can be as safe as possible.

26:40.240 --> 26:44.240
If you haven't spotted him yet, he's the guy with the red hair.

26:44.240 --> 26:48.240
Donald K. Deke Slayton, one of the original seven astronauts.

26:48.240 --> 26:51.240
At one time in his career was a member of the Minnesota Air National Guard.

26:51.240 --> 26:57.240
Deke has taken over the administrative responsibilities pertaining to all astronauts in NASA's program.

26:57.240 --> 27:04.240
Charles Conrad, Jr., astronaut who has orbited the Earth on two occasions and is raring to go again.

27:04.240 --> 27:07.240
He's a former member of the Pennsylvania Army National Guard.

27:07.240 --> 27:10.240
That's just a few. There are many others.

27:10.240 --> 27:33.240
Music

27:33.240 --> 27:38.240
Like the men you've just seen, however, guardsmen represent a cross section of the community.

27:38.240 --> 27:41.240
In reality, the Guard is the community.

27:41.240 --> 27:44.240
That's why it's an organization you can count on.

27:44.240 --> 27:49.240
The chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, General Earl Wheeler, once characterized the Guard by saying,

27:49.240 --> 27:54.240
From my long association with the National Guard, I've arrived at two conclusions.

27:54.240 --> 28:00.240
First, as a military organization, it is consistently better than the material with which it is equipped.

28:00.240 --> 28:07.240
Second, when the chips are down, the Guard surmounts whatever obstacles confront it and gets the job done.

28:07.240 --> 28:11.240
Well, the Guard's been that way for more than 330 years now.

28:11.240 --> 28:13.240
That's right, 330 years.

28:13.240 --> 28:16.240
Its past has been one of glory and accomplishment.

28:16.240 --> 28:19.240
But like that Saturn rocket, it has a long way to go.

28:19.240 --> 28:22.240
It'll reach higher and higher achievements in the future.

28:22.240 --> 28:27.240
This, then, is really not the end. It's just the beginning.

28:27.240 --> 28:51.240
♪♪

